 the offline algorithm  could go to the other point in the  domain  so this part incurs some tiny  movement costs for both the online and  offline algorithm because these points  are very close together in the middle  but now the transformation is applied  and when the transformation is applied  no additional cost is incurred by  definition of the problem  but the two online and offline locations  have been pulled apart  and this can now be abused by a request  sequence so if the next request forces  the online algorithm  to move all the way to the offline  algorithm then we see that the online  algorithm  pays a lot more movement cost than the  offline algorithm and we can  issue another relocate another  transformation that  moves the two points to the middle again  for free  so we've seen here that the online  algorithm pays a lot more than the  offline and therefore we can  get arbitrarily large lower ones on the  competitive ratio here  even on smaller spaces of five points or  even less point size  um it's possible to do this  and yeah of course we can repeat this as  often as we want  um yeah and therefore we get this lower  bound here so but what this really  relies on is that these transformations  uh pull these two locations of online  and offline very far apart  what is what would happen if if this  can't be the case  so say the transformations are always uh  non-expanding so there are one libgets  so as an example of this let's look at  chasing  contracting convex bodies so  what i mean by this is that the metric  space is a vector space  and every transformation is a  contraction between two convex sets  and uh if you want to test your  intuition then  feel free to pause the video for a  moment and have a guess of what you  expect the competitive ratio to be like  would it be harder than  convex body chasing or would it have the  same difficulty  well it turns out that maybe  surprisingly  the competitive ratio is infinite in  this case  and the reason it's infinite even in the  two-dimensional plane the reason  why we cannot just use the known  algorithms for convex body chasing and  and to modify them slightly  is that they rely on the fact that the  work function is convex  and this property will be destroyed if  we insert these contractions here  and yeah it is in indeed possible to  construct a lower bound showing that  there is no  competitive algorithm for this problem  so it may seem that we are doomed  but what's what's a bit special here  about this problem is that the metric  space contains infinitely many points  if we have a metric space of finitely  many points so let's say m is an  endpoint matrix space then  we can we can get a competitive  algorithm for the case of contractions  and even more generally  when all the transformations are alpha  elephants  so here we get a competitive ratio  that's roughly  alpha to the n if alpha is large enough  or otherwise  yeah six to the n this is a  deterministic algorithm  and there's also a lower bound that's um  well essentially matching if alpha is  large enough up to  some factor 2 in the  in front of the alpha here  and the lower bound is even working for  randomized algorithms  however note that if alpha is one so in  the case of non-expanding  isometries um non-non-expanding  transformations  then this lower bound is just one so  it's it doesn't give us anything  of course there's also the low one that  exists for medical service systems but  still there's a large  gap in the case of um  contractions and in particular also in  the case of  isometries between these upper and lower  bounds  so i'm going to show the ideas of this  upper bound proof  it consists of three steps the first  step is that we  define an ultrametric d head on  on the points of the metric space and  the head is going to be  a distortion of the true metric so it's  within a factor alpha plus one to the n  minus 2 of this metric and it will be  such that  any function that's alpha philips with  respect to d  is a contraction with respect to d hat  so we show that it's possible  to define such an ultrametric on any  finite metric  and alpha metric what this really means  is that it's a  special type of tree now the next step  is that we extend this tree this  ultrametric tree to  one that's completely symmetric so every  vertex  like any two vertices of the same level  have the same number of children  and the point of this now is that in  this symmetric ultrametric tree  we can adapt the analysis of the work  function algorithm for mss  to the case of tmss with  with these contractions so if it was  not contractions but isometries then  this would be  relatively simple because the isometries  extend to global isometries  in the case of contractions we make use  of some  properties that these ultrametrics have  that allow us to still analyze the work  function algorithm  so we lose the alpha plus one to the n  minus two factor in the competitive  ratio in the first step because this is  the error of the ultrametric compared to  the true metric  and we lose another factor of  roughly 2 to the n minus 1 because  we add so many more points in in the  second step where we extend the matrix  by  space  and uh yeah with that i'm coming to the  end so i'm just going to mention the  main  open problems so first of all what is  the competitive ratio of  tmss for interesting families of metric  spaces or transformations  so we think that isometries and  contractions are particularly  interesting transformations here because  we have  well first of all there are there are  natural classes  they occur in for instance the k taxi  problem  and the gaps between the upper and lower  ones that we have are very large here  and then there's this purely  mathematical question  given some finite metric space m what's  the minimal cardinality  of an extension m hat such that each  partial isometry on m  extends to automorphism on m hat  for this second question we also give  some partial answers for special cases  so for instance if m is an ultrametric  or if it's a general metric but we have  swap isometries so just swapping two  points  then the blow up here is at most  exponential  and in fact this is also tight for both  of these cases  but we don't have we don't know any  metric space where there's a larger  lower bound  and we also don't know an upper bound of  a similar form for general metric spaces  so this would be quite  interesting and i think of could be of  independent  interest so thanks a lot for watching  and goodbye
 

